<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<UserControl
    x:Class="Rudis.Shell.Regions.Canvas"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:regions="using:Rudis.Shell.Regions"
    FontFamily="Calibri">

    <!--
      Canvas — the region name is the handoff's, character-for-character
      (design_handoff_rudis_editor/README.md:33,96; CLAUDE.md rule 7 / D-19), and so
      are the five tool AutomationIds (README:41:
      `Canvas.ToolPalette.Pointer|Pen|Lasso|Text|Shape`).

      EVERY colour is a {StaticResource} token key from Theme/Tokens.xaml. A raw-hex
      literal here is a build FAILURE (MechanicalGatesTests.
      no_raw_hex_outside_the_token_dictionary, which enumerates shell/Rudis.Shell/**
      for .cs and .xaml).

      ═══ WHAT THIS REGION DOES, AND WHERE ═══

      TWO real ink surfaces, driven by ONE tool palette:

        · Canvas › ToolPalette drives DRAWING OVER THE PREVIEW through
          PreviewInkLayer, an ordinary transparent XAML sibling above the Preview
          region's SwapChainPanel (D-11). MainWindow wires ToolChanged ->
          PreviewRegion.Ink.SelectedTool. Those marks carry `space: "frame_linked"`
          and the ENGINE composites them onto the video.

        · The dotted-grid STAGE below is now the SECOND ink surface (plan 60.2-02,
          which RETIRES the Phase 51 scope note this paragraph used to carry). A
          drag on it with a draw tool selected becomes a REAL undoable
          `AddAnnotation` carrying `space: "whiteboard"` — dispatched through the
          same generic builder the Preview uses, with ZERO video loaded and ZERO
          composite ever having happened. Committed whiteboard marks are re-drawn
          here FROM THE MIRROR, so an Undo visibly removes one and a Redo restores
          it, with no stage-local retained state (CLAUDE.md rule 4). They NEVER
          composite into the Preview (T-51-13) — the engine draws `frame_linked`
          only — which is precisely why this region has to draw its own committed
          layer: it is the stage's half of D-13's trail hand-off.

      ═══ THE TWO BOUNDARIES THAT REMAIN, RECORDED RATHER THAN SMUGGLED ═══
      (repeated in Canvas.xaml.cs's class doc and in plan 60.2-02's SUMMARY)

      (1) The zoom chip stays an EXPLICITLY-LABELLED INERT PLACEHOLDER — see its own
          comment further down, which is unchanged. Canvas pan/zoom was offered to
          the owner on 2026-08-27 and DECLINED; there is no viewport transform
          anywhere in this region, and the stage box is simply the region's own
          bounds.

      (2) The agent's whiteboard raster is sized from the PREVIEW's aspect ratio
          (`whiteboard_raster_dims` in crates/ffi/src/ctx.rs), not from this stage's.
          Stage marks therefore reach the agent positionally correct in normalized
          space but ANISOTROPICALLY STRETCHED wherever the two aspects differ. That
          is an ACCEPTED, RECORDED limitation of Phase 60.2 (deferred-items D-1), not
          an oversight.
    -->

    <UserControl.Resources>

        <!-- ═══ One tile of Canvas › ToolPalette ═══
             Handoff *Tool-active* state (README:104): "active tool tile uses `accent`
             fill (others `text-secondary`)". Hover/disabled follow the same UI-SPEC
             gap resolutions Toolbar.xaml records for its own segmented control. -->
        <Style x:Key="CanvasToolStyle" TargetType="RadioButton">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-secondary}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="Width" Value="30" />
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="30" />
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualPrimaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource accent-bright}" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualSecondaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="RadioButton">
                        <Border x:Name="Fill"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-control}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal" />
                                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Glyph.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-primary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed" />
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Glyph.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-tertiary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CheckStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Unchecked" />
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Checked">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource accent}" />
                                            <Setter Target="Glyph.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Indeterminate" />
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                            </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Glyph"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                              Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                              FontSize="{TemplateBinding FontSize}"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- Stage input lands HERE, on the region root itself, and the handlers are
         wired in MARKUP so the region's input surface is visible where a reader
         looks first. `bg-app` is a real brush covering the whole region, which is
         what makes the root hit-testable at all (a null Brush is not). The tool
         palette and the zoom chip are declared AFTER the ink host below, so they
         stay above it and keep their own input; a RadioButton marks its pointer
         events handled, so a palette click never starts a stage gesture. -->
    <Grid x:Name="CanvasRoot"
          Background="{StaticResource bg-app}"
          SizeChanged="OnCanvasSizeChanged"
          PointerPressed="OnStagePointerPressed"
          PointerMoved="OnStagePointerMoved"
          PointerReleased="OnStagePointerReleased"
          PointerCaptureLost="OnStagePointerCaptureLost"
          PointerCanceled="OnStagePointerCaptureLost"
          AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.Root"
          AutomationProperties.Name="Canvas">

        <!-- The dotted-grid stage (README:96). Ordinary geometry on `border-hairline`
             — WinUI 3 has no tiling brush, so the dots are built once per size change
             in code-behind (a cold path: nothing here runs per frame). -->
        <Path x:Name="DotGrid"
              Fill="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
              IsHitTestVisible="False" />

        <!-- ═══ The stage's POINTER AFFORDANCE (D-60.2-12) ═══

             A full-bleed, otherwise-empty layer whose ONLY job is to carry a cursor:
             a crosshair while a draw tool is selected, nothing (i.e. the inherited
             default arrow) with `Pointer`. Canvas.xaml.cs's ApplyStageCursor decides,
             through InkCursorGate.WantsInkCursor — the same predicate PreviewInkLayer
             asks, so the two ink surfaces stay ONE product feature.

             It exists because the owner ran 60.2-HUMAN-UAT check 1 on a build whose
             stroke fidelity he PASSED and answered "still looks inert", reproducing
             his original "I can't draw on the canvas section" report: the stage was
             fully drawable and gave the pointer no cue at all, so the first feedback
             arrived only after he had already committed to a drag.

             THREE LOAD-BEARING FACTS, all of which a later edit can silently break:

             (i)   `Background="Transparent"` IS REQUIRED. A Grid with a null Brush
                   renders nothing and is not hit-testable, and an element that can
                   never BE the hit-test target can never contribute a cursor —
                   WinUI resolves the cursor by walking UP from the target. Same
                   lesson StageInkHost's comment records for its UIA rect, different
                   consequence.

             (ii)  IT MUST STAY DECLARED BELOW THE CHROME. The ToolPalette and the
                   zoom chip are declared LAST on purpose, so they sit above this
                   layer and take their own hits; their ancestor chain then carries
                   NO cursor anywhere and the arrow shows over them (true even of the
                   DISABLED chip — measured, see its own comment below).
                   Moving this element after them, or putting the cursor on
                   `CanvasRoot`/the UserControl instead, reintroduces exactly the
                   crosshair-over-the-toolbar defect this layer exists to avoid.
                   (`InkCursorWiringGateTests` pins that rule; `CanvasCursorUiaTests`
                   MEASURES it on the running shell.)

                   ⚠ AND Z-ORDER ALONE IS NOT THE WHOLE RULE (review WR-01). It
                   decides the CURSOR; it does NOT decide what a PRESS does, because
                   PointerPressed still bubbles from any chrome that does not mark it
                   handled and CanvasRoot's handler is wired for the whole region. So
                   THE CURSOR AND THE PRESS MUST BE DECIDED TOGETHER, PER ELEMENT, and
                   must agree — see OnChromePointerPressed in Canvas.xaml.cs for the
                   principle and each element below for its one-line justification.

             (iii) ITS POINTER EVENTS BUBBLE to CanvasRoot's markup-wired handlers —
                   which is the existing input design, unchanged. This layer marks
                   nothing handled and has no handlers of its own; `GetCurrentPoint`/
                   `GetIntermediatePoints` are taken relative to `CanvasRoot`
                   regardless of the hit-test target, nothing in Canvas.xaml.cs reads
                   `OriginalSource`, and capture stays on `CanvasRoot`.

             Deliberately NO AutomationProperties: this is not a contract surface.
             `Canvas.Stage` (StageInkHost) remains the UIA coordinate anchor, and is
             byte-untouched by this change. -->
        <regions:CursorHost x:Name="StageCursorLayer" Background="Transparent" />

        <!-- ═══ The stage's ink host ═══

             A DEDICATED AutomationId (on the host element below), rather than a
             reuse of `Canvas.Root`, is the DECIDED answer to this phase's research
             Open Question 2. The host is FULL-BLEED, so its UIA bounding rect IS
             the exact box `CanvasStageGesture.NormalizeInBox` divides by: a test
             that computes expected coordinates from the anchor's rect measures the
             same box the code normalized against. Precedent for two ids on one
             region: `Preview.Root` / `Preview.Surface`; and `Timeline.Root` proves
             a plain Grid is findable by `FindFirstDescendant`.

             ⚠ `Background="Transparent"` IS LOAD-BEARING, AND IT IS NOT ABOUT
             INPUT. Plan 60.2-04 MEASURED what a Background-less host reports to UI
             Automation on this machine: `IsOffscreen=True` and a bounding rectangle
             of `{0,0,0,0}` — an anchor that is findable and useless, which silently
             defeats the coordinate contract this element exists to provide. A Grid
             with a null Brush renders nothing, and WinUI's automation peer answers
             for what is RENDERED, not for what is laid out (`Canvas.Root` reports a
             real rect for exactly one reason: it carries `bg-app`). `Transparent` is
             a real brush, so the peer reports the real full-bleed rect
             (measured: host `608x241` inside root `608x242`, a 1px peer rounding at
             125% scale). Dropping `IsHitTestVisible="False"` was tried FIRST and
             changed nothing — hit-testability is not what makes the peer offscreen —
             so both attributes are needed and neither is redundant.

             ⚠ IsHitTestVisible="False" on the host AND on every child. Input must
             land on `CanvasRoot` itself (research Pattern 1): nothing beneath the
             stage needs pass-through, so — unlike PreviewInkLayer — there is no
             separate transparent sibling and no background toggling to gate the
             tool. And the drawn ink must never eat the pointer that is drawing it,
             which is the same rule PreviewInkLayer's LiveTrail states. This is also
             what makes the transparent brush above FREE: an element excluded from
             hit testing cannot intercept the pointer no matter what it is painted
             with, so the stage's gesture path is byte-unchanged by it.

             Declared AFTER `DotGrid` and BEFORE the chrome below: ink draws above
             the dots and beneath the region tag, palette and zoom chip.

             The ink styling — `accent`, 2px, dashed 4,3, round joins —
             byte-matches PreviewInkLayer's LiveTrail, so the two surfaces read as
             ONE product feature rather than two implementations. TOKENS ONLY: a raw
             hex literal anywhere in this file, comments included, is a build FAILURE
             (MechanicalGatesTests.no_raw_hex_outside_the_token_dictionary).

             `Path.Data` is REPLACED per update, never a `Polyline` whose `Points`
             collection is edited — plan 51-05's measured finding, restated on the
             elements it governs. See Canvas.xaml.cs's RenderStageTrail. -->
        <Grid x:Name="StageInkHost"
              Background="Transparent"
              IsHitTestVisible="False"
              AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.Stage"
              AutomationProperties.Name="Canvas stage">

            <!-- Every COMMITTED whiteboard mark, in ONE geometry and ONE brush,
                 rebuilt from the mirror per project:changed and per resize — a
                 cold path, never a per-frame one. -->
            <Path x:Name="CommittedInk"
                  Stretch="None"
                  Stroke="{StaticResource accent}"
                  StrokeThickness="2"
                  StrokeDashArray="4,3"
                  StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                  IsHitTestVisible="False" />

            <!-- Committed `label` marks. A Grid with Margin placement, NOT a XAML
                 `Canvas` panel: `Canvas` in this namespace is THIS region's own
                 class, so the panel's name would collide. -->
            <Grid x:Name="StageLabels" IsHitTestVisible="False" />

            <!-- The in-progress trail, drawn above the committed layer and HELD
                 after pointer-up until the mirror confirms (D-13). -->
            <Path x:Name="StageLiveTrail"
                  Stretch="None"
                  Stroke="{StaticResource accent}"
                  StrokeThickness="2"
                  StrokeDashArray="4,3"
                  StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                  IsHitTestVisible="False" />
        </Grid>

        <!-- Region tag. Handoff typography for region tags: 10px / 800 /
             letter-spacing .08em / `text-faint` (README:61-62). WinUI expresses
             letter-spacing in 1/1000 em, so .08em = 80.

             ⚠ IsHitTestVisible="False" IS THE CURSOR/PRESS DECISION FOR THIS ELEMENT
             (review WR-01), not a tidy-up. THE JUSTIFICATION, in one line: the tag is
             DECORATION — nobody aims at it — so it must not interrupt a stroke that
             crosses it. Being transparent to input, the hit falls through to
             StageCursorLayer, so the pointer shows the CROSSHAIR and the press draws:
             both halves agree. Before this attribute the TextBlock took the hit (so
             the pointer showed an ARROW, its ancestor chain carrying no cursor) while
             the press still bubbled to CanvasRoot and started a real undoable
             AddAnnotation — a cursor lying about what a click does, which is exactly
             the mirror-image defect InkCursorGate's own doc names.

             It does NOT hide the element from UI Automation: plan 60.2-04 MEASURED
             that on StageInkHost (hit-testability is not what makes a peer offscreen;
             a real Background is), and the tag's AutomationId is unchanged. -->
        <TextBlock Text="CANVAS"
                   Foreground="{StaticResource text-faint}"
                   FontSize="10"
                   FontWeight="ExtraBold"
                   CharacterSpacing="80"
                   Margin="16,12,0,0"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                   VerticalAlignment="Top"
                   IsHitTestVisible="False"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.RegionTag"
                   AutomationProperties.Name="Canvas region" />

        <!-- Canvas › ToolPalette — vertical, top-left (README:96).
             RadioButtons in one group: single-selection semantics AND arrow-key
             navigation for free, the same reasoning Toolbar.ModeTabs records.

             ⚠ PointerPressed IS THE CURSOR/PRESS DECISION FOR THIS ELEMENT (review
             WR-01). THE JUSTIFICATION, in one line: the palette is REAL CHROME the
             user aims at, so it shows the arrow — and it must therefore swallow the
             press, or the arrow is lying.

             The five RadioButton tiles already do that themselves (ButtonBase marks
             PointerPressed handled), which is why the one chrome reading the UIA test
             used to take — at Canvas.ToolPalette.Pen's centre — was the single place
             the claim cost nothing. THIS Border's own pixels are the ones that leaked:
             its 1px frame, its Padding="2" and the Spacing="2" gaps between the tiles
             belong to the Border and the StackPanel, neither of which handles a press,
             so a click on the toolbar drew a stroke under an arrow cursor. The handler
             sits on the Border because every one of those pixels hit-tests to it (the
             StackPanel paints no Background and so is never the target), and because a
             bubbling handler here also covers anything added inside it later. -->
        <Border x:Name="ToolPalette"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Margin="16,36,0,0"
                Background="{StaticResource bg-elevated}"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
                BorderThickness="1"
                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-control}"
                Padding="2"
                TabFocusNavigation="Once"
                PointerPressed="OnChromePointerPressed"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.ToolPalette"
                AutomationProperties.Name="Canvas tool palette">
            <StackPanel Orientation="Vertical" Spacing="2">
                <RadioButton x:Name="PointerTool"
                             Style="{StaticResource CanvasToolStyle}"
                             GroupName="CanvasToolPalette"
                             Content="&#x2316;"
                             IsChecked="True"
                             Checked="OnToolChecked"
                             AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.ToolPalette.Pointer"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="Pointer"
                             ToolTipService.ToolTip="Pointer — the ink layer is inert; clicks reach the video" />
                <RadioButton x:Name="PenTool"
                             Style="{StaticResource CanvasToolStyle}"
                             GroupName="CanvasToolPalette"
                             Content="&#x270E;"
                             Checked="OnToolChecked"
                             AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.ToolPalette.Pen"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="Pen"
                             ToolTipService.ToolTip="Pen — draw a freehand stroke over the video" />
                <RadioButton x:Name="LassoTool"
                             Style="{StaticResource CanvasToolStyle}"
                             GroupName="CanvasToolPalette"
                             Content="&#x25CC;"
                             Checked="OnToolChecked"
                             AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.ToolPalette.Lasso"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="Lasso"
                             ToolTipService.ToolTip="Lasso — enclose a region of the video (needs at least 3 points)" />
                <RadioButton x:Name="TextTool"
                             Style="{StaticResource CanvasToolStyle}"
                             GroupName="CanvasToolPalette"
                             Content="T"
                             Checked="OnToolChecked"
                             AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.ToolPalette.Text"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="Text"
                             ToolTipService.ToolTip="Text — place a label on the video" />
                <RadioButton x:Name="ShapeTool"
                             Style="{StaticResource CanvasToolStyle}"
                             GroupName="CanvasToolPalette"
                             Content="&#x25AD;"
                             Checked="OnToolChecked"
                             AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.ToolPalette.Shape"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="Shape"
                             ToolTipService.ToolTip="Shape — drag an arrow from where you press to where you release" />
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- The zoom chip (`− 100% +`, bottom-left — README:96).
             DELIBERATELY INERT AND DISABLED. The handoff ships it in the empty state,
             and CLAUDE.md rule 1 permits an explicitly-labelled placeholder where a
             FAKE OPERATION is not — the same standard `Inspector.Placeholder` already
             meets. Canvas pan/zoom is an infinite-surface feature with no backend
             behind it in this milestone; a chip that changed a number without zooming
             anything would be exactly the lie this project forbids.

             ⚠ THIS ELEMENT'S CURSOR/PRESS DECISION IS "NOTHING NEEDED", AND THAT WAS
             MEASURED RATHER THAN REASONED (review WR-01). WR-01 predicted that a
             Control with IsEnabled="False" is excluded from WinUI hit testing, so the
             pointer would fall straight THROUGH this chip to StageCursorLayer and show
             the CROSSHAIR over it — and an enabled wrapper was written and shipped to
             take the hit the disabled Button supposedly could not.

             THE PREDICTION IS FALSE on WinAppSDK 1.8, and the wrapper was REVERTED
             rather than kept as insurance. Measured by deleting it and re-running
             CanvasCursorUiaTests.the_cursor_and_the_press_agree_on_every_canvas_element
             against the bare chip: its centre reports IDC_ARROW (0x10003 — the same
             handle the palette tiles report, NOT the stage's IDC_CROSS 0x10009), and a
             full drag begun on it leaves Project.canvas.annotations EMPTY. A disabled
             Button therefore DOES take the hit and DOES consume the press: the cursor
             and the press already agree here, and no attribute can improve on that.

             The reading is a permanent probe in that test now, asserted in both halves
             like every other element — so if a future SDK does change how disabled
             controls hit-test, it fails there instead of being rediscovered as a
             mystery. The earlier claim in this file, in Canvas.xaml.cs and in
             InkCursorWiringGateTests' failure message that "the arrow shows over the
             zoom chip" turns out to have been RIGHT, for a reason nobody had checked. -->
        <Button x:Name="ZoomChip"
                IsEnabled="False"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                Margin="16,0,0,16"
                Background="{StaticResource bg-elevated}"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
                BorderThickness="1"
                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-control}"
                Padding="10,4,10,4"
                Content="&#x2212; 100% +"
                Foreground="{StaticResource text-tertiary}"
                FontSize="11"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Canvas.ZoomChip"
                AutomationProperties.Name="Canvas zoom"
                AutomationProperties.HelpText="Canvas pan/zoom is a deferred surface — this chip is inert"
                ToolTipService.ToolTip="Canvas pan/zoom is deferred; this chip is inert" />
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
